    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q1-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The results of  100 people taking part in a wine tasting survey are shown below. • 90 people liked wine A . • 90 people liked wine B . • 92 people liked wine C . • 88 people liked wine A and B . • 86 people liked wine B and C . • 87 people liked wine A and C . • 85 people liked wine A , B and C . a) Draw a fully completed Venn diagram to represent this data. ( ) 4\" style=\"--madas-width:568.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q1-question-2.svg\" alt=\"Find the probability that a randomly chosen person … b) … likes only two out of the three wines. ( ) 2 c) … likes wine B but not wine C . ( ) 2 d) … does not like wine B . ( ) 2 A person who likes at least two types of wine is selected. e) Determine the probability that this person likes wines A and C . ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:568.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q1-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Looking at the Venn diagram: A B 3 0 1 85 2 1 4 4 C (a) Start with the 85 people in all three sets: The two-wine-only regions AB, AC and BC have 88 −85 = 3, [B1] 87 −85 = 2 and 86 −85 = 1 [A1] respectively:\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q1-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"The single-wine regions then have 90 −(85 + 3 + 2) = 0, 90 −(85 + 3 + 1) = 1 [A1] and 92 −(85 + 2 + 1) = 4. The union has 96 people, leaving 4 outside all three sets: [M1] (b) The probability of exactly two wines is (3 + 2 + 1)/100 = 0.06 . [M1] [A1] (c) The probability of B but not C is (1 + 3)/100 = 0.04 . [M1] [A1] (d) The probability of disliking B is (2 + 4 + 4)/100, counting the regions outside B. Equivalently, 1 −90/100 = 0.10 . [M1] [A1] (e) The condition restricts the sample to 85 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 91 [B1] people: Of these, 85 + 2 = 87 [A1] like A and C, so the required probability is\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q1-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"3/100 + 2/100 + 1/100 + 85/100 = 87 2/100 + 85/100 91 ≈0.956. [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 1 is 13 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The results of  100 people taking part in a wine tasting survey are shown below. • 90 people liked wine A . • 90 people liked wine B . • 92 people liked wine C . • 88 people liked wine A and B . • 86 people liked wine B and C . • 87 people liked wine A and C . • 85 people liked wine A , B and C . a) Draw a fully completed Venn diagram to represent this data. ( ) 4 Find the probability that a randomly chosen person … b) … likes only two out of the three wines. ( ) 2 c) … likes wine B but not wine C . ( ) 2 d) … does not like wine B . ( ) 2 A person who likes at least two types of wine is selected. e) Determine the probability that this person likes wines A and C . ( ) 3"
  },
