    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-b/q5",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q5-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A continuous random variable X is Normally distributed with mean µ and standard deviation σ . It is further given that ( P X &gt; µ + a ) = 0.20 , where a is a positive constant. Determine ... a) ... ( P X &gt; µ − a ) . ( ) 1 b) ... ( P X − µ &lt; a ) . ( ) 2 c) ... ( P X &lt; µ + a X &gt; µ − a ) . ( ) 2\" style=\"--madas-width:572.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q5-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked solution diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:590.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q5-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"The normal distribution is symmetric about µ, so each outside tail beyond µ ± a has area 0.20. (a) P(X &gt; µ −a) = 1 −0.20 . [A1] = 0.80 (b) P(|X −µ| &lt; a) = P(µ −a &lt; X &lt; µ + a) = 1 −2(0.20) . [M1] [A1] = 0.60 (c) In the conditional probability, the intersection X &lt; µ + a and X &gt; µ −a is precisely the central band: Thus P((X &lt; µ + a) ∩(X &gt; µ −a)) = P(µ −a &lt; X &lt; µ + a) . P(X &gt; µ −a) P(X &gt; µ −a) Substitution gives P(X &lt; µ + a | X &gt; µ −a) = 0.60/0.80 . [M1] [A1] = 0.75\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 5 is 5 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "A continuous random variable X is Normally distributed with mean µ and standard deviation σ . It is further given that ( P X > µ + a ) = 0.20 , where a is a positive constant. Determine ... a) ... ( P X > µ − a ) . ( ) 1 b) ... ( P X − µ < a ) . ( ) 2 c) ... ( P X < µ + a X > µ − a ) . ( ) 2"
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-b/q6",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q6-question-1.svg\" alt=\"a) State four conditions for making a binomial distribution an appropriate model for statistical work. ( ) 4 Roy and Ned are door to door salesmen. The probability of Roy making a sale on a house visit is 0.05. b) Showing a clear method, find the probability that Roy achieves … i. … no sales in 15 visits. ( ) 2 ii. … more than 3 sales in 20 visits. ( ) 2 c) Calculate the least number of visits that Roy needs to make for the probability of at least 1 sale, to exceed 0.99 . ( ) 5\" style=\"--madas-width:572.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q6-question-2.svg\" alt=\"The probability of Ned making a sale on a house visit is 0.15. d) If Roy and Ned make 20 house visits each, determine the probability that they will make … i. … 2 sales each. ( ) 2 ii. … a total of 5 sales between them. ( ) 6 Ned makes 240 house visits in a given week. e) Use a Normal approximation, to find the probability that Ned achieves more than 30 sales in these 240 visits. ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:572.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) There is a fixed number of trials: [B1] Each trial has two possible outcomes: [A1] The probability of success is constant. [A1] The trials are independent: [B1] (b) For n visits, Roy’s sales count X has distribution B(n, 0.05). (i) ! 15 (0.05)0(0.95)15 P(X = 0) = 0 . [M1] [A1] = 0.9515 ≈0.4633 (ii) For n = 20, P(X &gt; 3) = P(X ≥4) = 1 −P(X ≤3) . [M1] [A1] 3 ! 20 0.05k0.9520−k ≈0.01590 = 1 − X k k=0\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(c) Require P(X ≥1) &gt; 0.99, so 1 −P(X = 0) &gt; 0.99. (0.05)0(0.95)n, this is 1 −0.95n &gt; 0.99, [B1] so 0.95n &lt; 0.01. [A1] n Since P(X = 0) = 0 Taking logarithms gives n log(0.95) &lt; log(0.01). Division by the negative number log(0.95) reverses the inequality, giving n &gt; log(0.01)/ log(0.95) ≈89.78. [A1] The least number of visits is 90 . [M1] [A1] (d) Let X ∼B(20, 0.05) and Y ∼B(20, 0.15) be Roy’s and Ned’s sales counts: Treat the two counts as independent: (i) ! ! 20 20 0.1520.8518 ≈0.04327 . 0.0520.9518 P(X = 2) P(Y = 2) = [M1] [A1] 2 2 (ii) List the six pairs (k, 5 −k) with five sales in total: [B1] For each k = 0, . . . , 5, calculate (0.05)k(0.95)20−k and 20 P(X = k) = k\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q6-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"20 ! (0.15)k(0.85)20−k. P(Y = k) = k For example, P(X = 0) = 0.9520 = 0.35849 . . . and ! 20 P(Y = 5) = (0.15)5(0.85)15 5 . = 0.10285 . . . Use the binomial model for Roy’s probabilities [M1] and for Ned’s probabilities, [A1] then multiply within each pair by independence: [A1] k P(X = k) 5 −k P(Y = 5 −k) product 0 0.35849 5 0.10285 0.03687 5 0.00224 0 0.03876 0.00009 1 0.37735 4 0.18212 0.06872 4 0.01333 1 0.13680 0.00182\" style=\"--madas-width:653.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q6-solution-4.svg\" alt=\"2 0.18868 3 0.24283 0.04582 3 0.05958 2 0.22934 0.01366 Sum the products for the six disjoint outcomes: [A1] Using unrounded values gives 0.16698 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:669.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-b/q6-solution-5.svg\" alt=\"(e) With 240 visits, Y ∼B(240, 0.15) [B1] [A1] has mean 240(0.15) = 36 and variance 36(0.85) = 30.6. [M1] [A1] Since Y is integer-valued, Y &gt; 30 is Y ≥31. For W ∼N(36, 30.6) use the continuity correction: 30.5 −36  P(Y &gt; 30) ≈P(W &gt; 30.5) = 1 −Φ √ = Φ(0.9943 . . .) ≈0.8400 . [M1] [A1] 30.6 Source note. The handwritten model label in part (e) gives the mean as 30. Its working uses the correct mean of 36.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 27 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "a) State four conditions for making a binomial distribution an appropriate model for statistical work. ( ) 4 Roy and Ned are door to door salesmen. The probability of Roy making a sale on a house visit is 0.05. b) Showing a clear method, find the probability that Roy achieves … i. … no sales in 15 visits. ( ) 2 ii. … more than 3 sales in 20 visits. ( ) 2 c) Calculate the least number of visits that Roy needs to make for the probability of at least 1 sale, to exceed 0.99 . ( ) 5 The probability of Ned making a sale on a house visit is 0.15. d) If Roy and Ned make 20 house visits each, determine the probability that they will make … i. … 2 sales each. ( ) 2 ii. … a total of 5 sales between them. ( ) 6 Ned makes 240 house visits in a given week. e) Use a Normal approximation, to find the probability that Ned achieves more than 30 sales in these 240 visits. ( ) 6"
